## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors of Things that Go – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Red Bike.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name common objects and people, including family members. Interactive: Take part in very simple classroom games using one or two repeated lines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can name transportation vehicles using color words (e.g., "That's a green car."). Can answer simple questions about transportation and colors using one or two words (e.g., "What color is the car?"). Can participate in classroom activities involving transportation vocabulary (e.g., "naming vehicles").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Color Chant and Movement" activity. Students stand up. The teacher calls out a color (e.g., "Red!"), and students repeat "Red!" while pointing to something red in the classroom or wearing red. This is repeated with other target colors (blue, yellow, green, purple, orange) using TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of colored transportation vehicles (e.g., a red bike, a green car, a blue bus). The teacher clearly models phrases like, "Look! That's a red bike." and "It is a green car." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /k/ in "car," /b/ in "bus," and /y/ in "yellow," encouraging choral repetition. The teacher then models asking, "What color is this car?" and answering, "It's a green car."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple visual aids and gestures to check understanding.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a blue bus flashcard and asks, "Is this a red bus? (Yes/No gesture)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a yellow taxi and asks, "What is this? (Students point or say 'taxi')."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A sequence of large, colorful picture cards showing different transportation vehicles (car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi) each in a different color. A simple, repetitive oral story/dialogue led by the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects: When describing colors in transportation, use phrases that describe vehicles, like "It is a red bike." "That is a green car." or "It's a blue bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Presentation Activity: "Our Colorful Vehicles Story"</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents a "story" using the large picture cards. For example, "Look! I see a red car. (Teacher points and emphasizes 'red car')." "And here, I see a blue bus." The teacher repeats these phrases for each vehicle.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then varies the sentences: "That is a yellow taxi." "It's a green train."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and point to the corresponding picture card as the teacher names it. They are encouraged to repeat the phrases chorally: "A red car!", "A blue bus!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the green car!" (Students point to the correct flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me the red bike!" (Students identify and point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a blue truck and asks, "Is this a blue truck?" (Students nod or shake head, or say "Yes!"/"No!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Color + noun (e.g., "green car"), WH-questions (e.g., "What color is the car?"), Present simple (e.g., "It is a red bike.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi, green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange, this, that.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /k/ in "car", /b/ in "bus", /y/ in "yellow".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing objects, Answering questions about colors and objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Using "Please" for requests.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Echo the Color Car"</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., a purple truck) and clearly says, "It's a purple truck."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students chorally repeat, "It's a purple truck."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher repeats this with all target vehicle flashcards and colors, ensuring clear pronunciation of /k/, /b/, /y/ sounds.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For variety, the teacher can ask individual students to repeat after the group.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "What Color Is It?" Chain Drill</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., a red bike) and asks the class, "What color is the bike?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students respond chorally, "It's a red bike!" (or simply "Red bike!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher passes the flashcard to the first student, who then asks the next student, "What color is the bike?" The second student answers, and the chain continues. The teacher monitors closely for accuracy.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher can interject with "Please, tell me the color of the car." to embed politeness norms.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Find My Vehicle"</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of shuffled mini-flashcards (or drawings) of colored vehicles.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student asks, "What do you see?" or "Please, show me a green car." The other student finds the correct card and says, "It's a green car!" or "Here's a green car!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They take turns asking and answering, practicing the target phrases and questions. The teacher circulates, providing support and correction as needed.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Transportation Traffic Jam" Game</b> (Collaborative interaction and descriptive speaking)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher lays out large pictures of a "road" or "city scene" on the floor or a large table.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a small picture card of a colored transportation vehicle (e.g., a yellow bus, a blue taxi, an orange truck).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stand around the "road." The teacher acts as the "traffic controller."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out, "Who has a red bike? Please, drive your red bike to the blue building!" The student with the red bike holds it up and says, "I have a red bike!" and "drives" it to the specified location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can also take turns being the "traffic controller," asking peers, "What color is your car?" or "Please, go with the green bus!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on students actively producing the target phrases "I have a [color] [vehicle]," "It's a [color] [vehicle]," and responding to "What color is...?" questions, as well as following simple instructions involving colors and vehicles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages interaction: "Ask Maria what color her truck is." (e.g., "Maria, what color is your truck?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Transportation Traffic Jam" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Transportation Traffic Jam" game, demonstrating their ability to name colored transportation vehicles and answer simple questions about them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (colors and vehicles).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar (e.g., "It's a [color] [vehicle]").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer "What color is the...?" questions with one or two words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaborative interaction in the game.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of politeness norms (e.g., responding to "Please, point...").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down if they can say "a red car" and "a blue bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students point to a colored vehicle flashcard and say its name and color to a partner or the teacher before leaving the activity space.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new color and vehicle words can I say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I like playing the traffic jam game with my friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What activities worked well for promoting speaking and interaction in this 1st-grade class?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to use the target phrases ("It's a [color] [vehicle]") and answer simple "What color is...?" questions effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more visual cues, simpler instructions, more one-on-one pronunciation practice) are needed for the next speaking opportunity?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the pronunciation focus on /k/, /b/, /y/ contribute to clarity?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will apply their knowledge of colors and transportation vehicles to a simple writing composition task, perhaps by drawing and labeling a colored vehicle or completing a simple sentence about it.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ </p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>